m, and then apply the first isomorphism theorem.\n\nLet $G$ be an abelian group, and consider the sets $H = \\{g^2 \\mid g \\in G\\}$ and $K = \\{g \\in G \\mid g^2 = e\\}$. The set $K$ is the kernel of the homomorphism $f : G \\to G$ defined by $f(g) = g^2$. By the first isomorphism theorem, we have $G/\\ker(f) \\cong \\text{im}(f)$. Since $\\ker(f) = K$ and $\\text{im}(f) = H$, we conclude that $G/K \\cong H$.\n\n\\boxed{G/K \\cong H}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Let $G$ be an abelian group, and consider the sets $H = \\{g^2 \\mid g \\in G\\}$ and $K = \\{g \\in G \\mid g^2 = e\\}$. Define the homomorphism $f : G \\to G$ by $f(g) = g^2$. Use the first isomorphism theorem to show that $G/K \\cong H$. Provide a detailed explanation of your reasoning.\nassistant\n", "response": "To show that $G/K \\cong H$, we will use the first isomorphism theorem. The first isomorphism theorem states that if $f : G \\to G'$ is a group homomorphism, then $G/\\ker(f) \\cong \\text{im}(f)$, where $\\ker(f)$ is the kernel of $f$ and $\\text{im}(f)$ is the image of $f$.\n\nIn our case, we have the homomorphism $f : G \\to G$ defined by $f(g) = g^2$. We need to find the kernel and image of $f$.\n\nThe kernel of $f$, denoted as $\\ker(f)$, is the set of all elements $g \\in G$ such that $f(g) = e$, where $e$ is the identity element in $G$. So, $\\ker(f) = \\{g \\in G \\mid g^2 = e\\}$. This is exactly the set $K$ we defined earlier.\n\nThe image of $f$, denoted as $\\text{im}(f)$, is the set of all elements $h \\in G$ such that there exists an element $g \\in G$ with $f(g) = h$. So, $\\text{im}(f) = \\{g^2 \\mid g \\in G\\}$. This is exactly the set $H$ we defined earlier.\n\nNow, we can apply the first isomorphism theorem: $G/\\ker(f) \\cong \\text{im}(f)$. Since $\\ker(f) = K$ and $\\text{im}(f) = H$, we have $G/K \\cong H$.\n\nThus, \\boxed{G/K \\cong H}."}, {"prompt": 