\\(I = (a)\\).\n\n2. **Definition of a Noetherian Ring**: A ring is Noetherian if it satisfies the ascending chain condition (ACC) on ideals. This means that any ascending chain of ideals \\(I_1 \\subseteq I_2 \\subseteq I_3 \\subseteq \\cdots\\) eventually stabilizes, i.e., there exists an integer \\(n\\) such that \\(I_n = I_{n+1} = I_{n+2} = \\cdots\\).\n\n3. **Relating Principal Rings to Noetherian Rings**: Since every ideal in a principal ring is principal, we can consider an ascending chain of ideals in such a ring. Let's say we have an ascending chain of ideals: \\(I_1 \\subseteq I_2 \\subseteq I_3 \\subseteq \\cdots\\). Each \\(I_i\\) is generated by a single element, say \\(I_i = (a_i)\\).\n\n4. **Stabilization of the Chain**: Because the ideals are principal, the elements generating them must eventually be related in a way that stabilizes the chain. Specifically, there must exist an integer \\(n\\) such that for all \\(m \\geq n\\), \\(a_m\\) is contained in the ideal generated by \\(a_n\\). This is because in a principal ring, if \\(a_m\\) is not contained in \\((a_n)\\) for all \\(m\\), then the chain would not terminate, contradicting the fact that the chain must terminate due to the properties of principal ideals.\n\n5. **Conclusion**: Since every ascending chain of ideals in a principal ring eventually stabilizes, a principal ring satisfies the ascending chain condition and is therefore Noetherian.\n\nThus, the statement \"A principal ring, where every ideal is generated by a single element, is also Noetherian\" is true.\n\n\\boxed{\\text{true}}"}]
