omorphism between \\( A \\) and \\( B \\).\n\n1. **Bijection and Homomorphism:**\n   - \\(\\phi_1\\) is a bijection, which means it is both injective and surjective.\n   - \\(\\phi_2\\) is a homomorphism, which means it preserves the group operation.\n\n2. **Combining the Properties:**\n   - For \\(\\phi_1\\) to be an isomorphism, it must also be a homomorphism.\n   - \\(\\phi_1\\) is already a bijection, so we just need to check if it is a homomorphism.\n\n3. **Checking if \\(\\phi_1\\) is a Homomorphism:**\n   - Since \\(\\phi_2\\) is a homomorphism and \\(\\phi_1 \\neq \\phi_2\\), it does not necessarily mean that \\(\\phi_1\\) is not a homomorphism. However, we do not have any information about the group operations being preserved by \\(\\phi_1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - Without additional information about whether \\(\\phi_1\\) preserves the group operation, we cannot definitively conclude that \\(\\phi_1\\) is an isomorphism.\n   - Therefore, it is possible that no isomorphism exists between \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) if \\(\\phi_1\\) is not a homomorphism.\n\nThus, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{No}}\\)."}]
