han 2.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the integral \\( \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\), where \\( h \\) is an entire function, \\( C \\) is the circle centered at the origin with radius 1, and \\( m \\) is a positive integer greater than 2, we can use the residue theorem. The residue theorem states that for a function \\( f(z) \\) that is meromorphic inside and on a simple closed contour \\( C \\),\n\n\\[ \\int_{C} f(z) \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\sum \\text{Res}(f, a_k) \\]\n\nwhere the sum is over all the residues of \\( f(z) \\) at its poles inside \\( C \\).\n\nIn our case, the function is\n\n\\[ f(z) = \\frac{h(z)}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)}. \\]\n\nThe poles of \\( f(z) \\) are at \\( z = 2, 3, \\ldots, m \\). Since the contour \\( C \\) is the circle centered at the origin with radius 1, none of these poles lie inside \\( C \\). Therefore, there are no poles of \\( f(z) \\) inside the contour \\( C \\).\n\nSince there are no poles inside the contour \\( C \\), the sum of the residues inside \\( C \\) is zero. Therefore, by the residue theorem,\n\n\\[ \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} = 2\\pi i \\sum \\text{Res}(f, a_k) = 2\\pi i \\cdot 0 = 0. \\]\n\nThus, the value of the integral is\n\n\\[ \\boxed{0}. \\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the integral \\( \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\) where \\( h \\) is an entire function, \\( C \\) is the circle centered at the origin with radius 1, and \\( m \\) is a positive integer greater than 2.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the integral \\( \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\), we can use the Cauchy Integral Formula. The Cauchy Integral Formula states that for a function \\( f(z) \\) that is analytic inside and on a simple closed curve \\( C \\), and for any point \\( a \\) inside \\( C \\),\n\n\\[ f(a) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i} \\int_{C} \\frac{f(z)}{z-a} \\, dz. \\]\n\nIn our case, the function \\( f(z) = \\frac{h(z)}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\) is analytic inside and on the circle \\( C \\) because the singularities \\( z=2, z=3, \\ldots, z=m \\) are all outside the circle \\( C \\) since \\( C \\) is the circle centered at the origin with radius 1 and \\( m > 2 \\).\n\nThe function \\( h(z) \\) is an entire function, meaning it is analytic everywhere. Therefore, \\( f(z) \\) has no singularities inside \\( C \\). According to the Cauchy Integral Theorem, if a function is analytic inside and on a simple closed curve, then the integral of the function over that curve is zero. Hence,\n\n\\[ \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} = 0. \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is\n\n\\[ \\boxed{0}. \\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the integral \\( \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\) where \\( h \\) is an entire function, \\( C \\) is the circle centered at the origin with radius 1, and \\( m \\) is a positive integer greater than 2.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the integral \\( \\int_{C} \\frac{h(z) \\, dz}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\), we will use the Residue Theorem. The Residue Theorem states that for a function \\( f(z) \\) that is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour \\( C \\), except for a finite number of singular points inside \\( C \\), the integral of \\( f(z) \\) around \\( C \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\int_{C} f(z) \\, dz = 2\\pi i \\sum \\text{Res}(f, a_k) \\]\n\nwhere the sum is over all the singular points \\( a_k \\) inside \\( C \\).\n\nIn our case, the function \\( f(z) = \\frac{h(z)}{(z-2)(z-3) \\cdots (z-m)} \\) has singular points at \\( z = 2, 3, \\ldots, m \\). Since the contour \\( C \\) is the circle centered at the origin with radius 1, none of these