ifferentiating both sides of the equation \\( x + \\int_{0}^{x} f(t) \\, dt = e^x - 1 \\) with respect to \\( x \\).\n\nThe left-hand side is:\n\\[ \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( x + \\int_{0}^{x} f(t) \\, dt \\right) = 1 + f(x) \\]\n\nThe right-hand side is:\n\\[ \\frac{d}{dx} (e^x - 1) = e^x \\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[ 1 + f(x) = e^x \\]\n\\[ f(x) = e^x - 1 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the interval representing the set of all \\( x \\) such that \\( 1 \\leq f(x) \\leq 2 \\). Substituting \\( f(x) = e^x - 1 \\) into the inequality, we get:\n\\[ 1 \\leq e^x - 1 \\leq 2 \\]\n\nAdding 1 to all parts of the inequality, we obtain:\n\\[ 2 \\leq e^x \\leq 3 \\]\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of all parts, we get:\n\\[ \\ln(2) \\leq x \\leq \\ln(3) \\]\n\nTherefore, the interval representing the set of all \\( x \\) such that \\( 1 \\leq f(x) \\leq 2 \\) is \\([ \\ln(2), \\ln(3) ]\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[\\ln(2), \\ln(3)]}\n\\]"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. For a continuous function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the equation \\( x+\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt=e^x-1 \\) for all \\( x\\in \\mathbb{R} \\), find the interval representing the set of all \\( x \\) such that \\( 1\\leq f(x)\\leq 2 \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To solve the given problem, we start with the functional equation:\n\n\\[ x + \\int_{0}^{x} f(t) \\, dt = e^x - 1. \\]\n\nFirst, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{d}{dx} \\left( x + \\int_{0}^{x} f(t) \\, dt \\right) = \\frac{d}{dx} (e^x - 1). \\]\n\nThe left-hand side simplifies using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus:\n\n\\[ 1 + f(x) = e^x. \\]\n\nSolving for \\( f(x) \\), we get:\n\n\\[ f(x) = e^x - 1. \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the interval for \\( x \\) such that \\( 1 \\leq f(x) \\leq 2 \\). Substituting \\( f(x) = e^x - 1 \\) into the inequality, we have:\n\n