ersely as \\( x \\). If \\( y = -4 \\) when \\( x = 7 \\), find \\( x \\) when \\( y = -3 \\).\nedassistant\nSince \\( y \\) varies inversely as \\( x \\), we can write the relationship as:\n\\[ y = \\frac{k}{x} \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is a constant. To find \\( k \\), we use the given values \\( y = -4 \\) and \\( x = 7 \\):\n\\[ -4 = \\frac{k}{7} \\]\nSolving for \\( k \\), we multiply both sides by 7:\n\\[ k = -4 \\times 7 = -28 \\]\nSo the equation that describes the relationship between \\( y \\) and \\( x \\) is:\n\\[ y = \\frac{-28}{x} \\]\nNow, we need to find \\( x \\) when \\( y = -3 \\). We substitute \\( y = -3 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ -3 = \\frac{-28}{x} \\]\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we multiply both sides by \\( x \\):\n\\[ -3x = -28 \\]\nThen, we divide both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{-28}{-3} = \\frac{28}{3} \\]\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) when \\( y = -3 \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{28}{3}} \\]"}]
