lue for $-x^2- 6x + 12$?\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the value of \\( x \\) that maximizes the quadratic expression \\( -x^2 - 6x + 12 \\), we can use the vertex formula for a parabola. The general form of a quadratic equation is \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\), and the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the vertex is given by \\( x = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\).\n\nIn the given expression \\( -x^2 - 6x + 12 \\), we have \\( a = -1 \\), \\( b = -6 \\), and \\( c = 12 \\). Plugging these values into the vertex formula, we get:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{-6}{2(-1)} = \\frac{6}{-2} = -3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) that will give the maximum value for \\( -x^2 - 6x + 12 \\) is \\( \\boxed{-3} \\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. What value of $x$ will give the maximum value for $-x^2- 6x + 12$?\nassistant\n", "response": "The expression \\(-x^2 - 6x + 12\\) is a quadratic equation in the form of \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\), where \\(a = -1\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 12\\). The maximum or minimum value of a quadratic function \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) occurs at \\(x = -\\frac{b}{2a}\\). \n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we can find the value of \\(x\\) that will give the maximum value as follows:\n\\[ x = -\\frac{b}{2a} = -\\frac{-6}{2(-1)} = -\\frac{6}{2} = -3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x\\) that will give the maximum value for \\(-x^2 - 6x + 12\\) is \\(\\boxed{-3}.\\)ed\n\\end{document}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. The six faces of a three-inch wooden cube are each painted red. The cube is then cut into one-inch cubes along the lines shown in the diagram. How many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces? [asy]\n\npair A,B,C,D,E,F,G;\n\npair a,c,d,f,g,i,j,l,m,o,p,r,s,u,v,x,b,h;\n\nA=(0.8,1);\nB=(0,1.2);\nC=(1.6,1.3);\nD=(0.8,0);\nE=B-(A-D);\nF=C-(A-D);\nG=B+(C-A);\n\ndraw(E--D--F--C--G--B--A--D);\ndraw(A--C); draw(E--B);\n\na=(1/3)*D+(2/3)*E;\nc=(2/3)*D+(1/3)*E;\np=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*B;\nr=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*B;\n\ndraw(a--p);\ndraw(c--r);\n\nv=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*E;\nx=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*E;\nb=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*D;\nh=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(v--b);\ndraw(x--h);\n\ns=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*A;\nu=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*A;\nd=(1/3)*F+(2/3)*D;\nf=(2/3)*F+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(s--d);\ndraw(f--u);\n\ng=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*F;\ni=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*F;\n\ndraw(i--h);\ndraw(g--b);\n\nm=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*G;\no=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*G;\n\ndraw(m--u);\ndraw(o--s);\n\nj=(1/3)*G+(2/3)*C;\nl=(2/3)*G+(1/3)*C;\n\ndraw(l--p);\ndraw(r--j);\n\n[/asy]\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces, we need to analyze the structure of the original cube and the smaller cubes after it is cut.\n\nThe original cube is a 3-inch cube, and when it is cut into one-inch cubes, we get a 3x3x3 grid of smaller cubes, which is a total of \\(3^3 = 27\\) smaller cubes.\n\nWe will categorize the smaller cubes based on how many faces are painted:\n1. **Cubes with 3 faces painted**: These are the corner cubes of the original cube. There are 8 such cubes.\n2. **Cubes with 2 faces painted**: These are the cubes on the edges of the original cube, excluding the corners. Each edge of the original cube is 3 inches long, so when cut, it produces 3 smaller cubes, but the two at the ends are corners. Therefore, each edge has \\(3-2=1\\) cube with 2 faces painted. Since a cube has 12 edges, there are \\(12 \\times 1 = 12\\) such cubes.\n3. **Cubes with 1 face painted**: These are the cubes on the faces of the original cube, excluding the edges. Each face of the original cube is a 3x3 grid of smaller cubes, so it has \\(3^2 = 9\\) smaller cubes. However, the outermost row and column are on the edges, so each face has \\((3-2)^2 = 1\\) cube with 1 face painted. Since a cube has 6 faces, there are \\(6 \\times 1 = 6\\) such cubes.\n4. **Cubes with 0 faces painted**: These are the cubes inside the original cube, not on any face. There is only one such cube, which is the center cube.\n\nWe are interested in the cubes that have red paint on at least two faces. These are the cubes with 3 faces painted and the cubes with 2 faces painted. From the categorization above, there are 8 cubes with 3 faces painted and 12 cubes with 2 faces painted.\n\nTherefore, the total number of cubes with red paint on at least two faces is \\(8 + 12 = 20\\).\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. The six faces of a three-inch wooden cube are each painted red. The cube is then cut into one-inch cubes along the lines shown in the diagram. How many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces? [asy]\n\npair A,B,C,D,E,F,G;\n\npair a,c,d,f,g,i,j,l,m,o,p,r,s,u,v,x,b,h;\n\nA=(0.8,1);\nB=(0,1.2);\nC=(1.6,1.3);\nD=(0.8,0);\nE=B-(A-D);\nF=C-(A-D);\nG=B+(C-A);\n\ndraw(E--D--F--C--G--B--A--D);\ndraw(A--C); draw(E--B);\n\na=(1/3)*D+(2/3)*E;\nc=(2/3)*D+(1/3)*E;\np=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*B;\nr=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*B;\n\ndraw(a--p);\ndraw(c--r);\n\nv=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*E;\nx=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*E;\nb=(1/3)*A+(2/3)*D;\nh=(2/3)*A+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(v--b);\ndraw(x--h);\n\ns=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*A;\nu=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*A;\nd=(1/3)*F+(2/3)*D;\nf=(2/3)*F+(1/3)*D;\n\ndraw(s--d);\ndraw(f--u);\n\ng=(1/3)*C+(2/3)*F;\ni=(2/3)*C+(1/3)*F;\n\ndraw(i--h);\ndraw(g--b);\n\nm=(1/3)*B+(2/3)*G;\no=(2/3)*B+(1/3)*G;\n\ndraw(m--u);\ndraw(o--s);\n\nj=(1/3)*G+(2/3)*C;\nl=(2/3)*G+(1/3)*C;\n\ndraw(l--p);\ndraw(r--j);\n\n[/asy]\nassistant\n", "response": "To determine how many of the one-inch cubes have red paint on at least two faces, we need to analyze the structure of the larger cube after it is cut into smaller cubes.\n\nThe larger cube is a 3x3x3 cube, which means it is cut into \\(3^3 = 27\\) smaller one-inch cubes.\n\n### Step 1: Identify the types of smaller cubes\nThe smaller cubes can be categorized based on the number of faces that are painted:\n- **Corner cubes**: These cubes have 3 faces painted. There are 8 corner cubes in a 3x3x3 cube.\n- **Edge cubes**: These cubes have 2 faces painted. There are 12 edges in a cube, and each edge contains 3 smaller cubes, but the two at the ends are corner cubes. Therefore, each edge has \\(3-2=1\\) edge cube, and there are \\(12 \\times 1 = 12\\) edge cubes.\n- **Face center cubes**: These cubes have 1 face painted. There are 6 faces, and each face is a 3x3 grid of smaller cubes. The center 1x1 grid (the face center) has 1 cube, and there are \\(6 \\times 1 = 6\\) face center cubes.\n- **Interior cubes**: These cubes have no faces painted. There is a 1x1x1 cube in the center of the 3x3x3 cube, which is \\(1^3 = 1\\) interior cube.\n\n### Step 2: Count the cubes with at least two faces painted\nThe cubes with at least two faces painted are the corner cubes and the edge cubes:\n- **Corner cubes**: 8\n- **Edge cubes**: 12\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\\[ 8 + 12 = 20 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of one-inch cubes that have red paint on at least two faces is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."}]
