inal response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the limit \\( \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty}\\int _{a}^b f(t)\\sin(nt)\\,\\mathrm dt \\) where \\( f \\) is a continuous function on \\([a, b]\\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the limit \\( \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty}\\int _{a}^b f(t)\\sin(nt)\\,\\mathrm dt \\) where \\( f \\) is a continuous function on \\([a, b]\\), we can use the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma. The Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma states that if \\( f \\) is an integrable function on \\([a, b]\\), then\n\n\\[ \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty} \\int_{a}^{b} f(t) \\sin(nt) \\, \\mathrm{d}t = 0. \\]\n\nSince \\( f \\) is continuous on \\([a, b]\\), it is also integrable on \\([a, b]\\). Therefore, by the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma, we have\n\n\\[ \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty}\\int _{a}^b f(t)\\sin(nt)\\,\\mathrm dt = 0. \\]\n\nSo the limit is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).ed\n\\end{document}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Evaluate the limit \\( \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty}\\int _{a}^b f(t)\\sin(nt)\\,\\mathrm dt \\) where \\( f \\) is a continuous function on \\([a, b]\\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate the limit \\( \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty}\\int _{a}^b f(t)\\sin(nt)\\,\\mathrm dt \\), we can use the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma. The Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma states that if \\( f \\) is an integrable function on \\([a, b]\\), then\n\n\\[ \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int_a^b f(t) \\sin(nt) \\, dt = 0. \\]\n\nSince \\( f \\) is a continuous function on \\([a, b]\\), it is also integrable on \\([a, b]\\). Therefore, we can apply the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma to conclude that\n\n\\[ \\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty}\\int _{a}^b f(t)\\sin(nt)\\,\\mathrm dt = 0. \\]\n\nThus, the limit is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).ed\n\\end{document}"}]
