\$24.50 \\]\n\nThe total cost to cover the room with carpet and padding is:\n\\[ 30 \\text{ square yards} \\times \\$24.50 \\text{ per square yard} = \\$735 \\]\n\nThus, the cost of the carpet and padding needed to exactly cover the room is \\(\\boxed{735}\\).ed\n\\end{document}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Find the limit of the sequence \\(a_n = \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n}\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity using the sandwich theorem.\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the limit of the sequence \\(a_n = \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n}\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity, we can use the sandwich theorem. First, observe that the term \\(n^n\\) dominates the sum \\(1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n\\) as \\(n\\) grows large. Therefore, we can establish the following inequalities:\n\n\\[n^n \\leq 1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n \\leq n \\cdot n^n.\\]\n\nTaking the \\(n\\)-th root of each part of the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\\sqrt[n]{n^n} \\leq \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n} \\leq \\sqrt[n]{n \\cdot n^n}.\\]\n\nSimplifying the left and right sides, we have:\n\n\\[n \\leq a_n \\leq \\sqrt[n]{n} \\cdot n.\\]\n\nAs \\(n\\) approaches infinity, \\(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\) approaches 1. Therefore, the right side of the inequality approaches \\(n\\). By the sandwich theorem, we conclude that \\(a_n\\) approaches \\(n\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity. Thus, the limit of the sequence \\(a_n\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity is \\(\\boxed{n}\\).ed\n\\end{document}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Find the limit of the sequence \\(a_n = \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n}\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity using the sandwich theorem.\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the limit of the sequence \\(a_n = \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n}\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity, we can use the sandwich theorem. First, observe that the term \\(n^n\\) dominates the sum \\(1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n\\) as \\(n\\) becomes large. Therefore, we can bound \\(a_n\\) from below and above by expressions that are easier to handle.\n\nLower bound:\n\\[a_n = \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{n^n} = n.\\]\n\nUpper bound:\n\\[a_n = \\sqrt[n]{1^n + 2^n + \\cdots + n^n} \\leq \\sqrt[n]{n \\cdot n^n} = \\sqrt[n]{n^{n+1}} = n \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n}.\\]\n\nAs \\(n\\) approaches infinity, \\(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\) approaches 1. Therefore, the upper bound \\(n \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n}\\) approaches \\(n\\). We have:\n\\[n \\leq a_n \\leq n \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n}.\\]\n\nBy the sandwich theorem, since both the lower and upper bounds approach \\(n\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity, we conclude that \\(a_n\\) also approaches \\(n\\). Therefore, the limit of the sequence \\(a_n\\) as \\(n\\) approaches infinity is \\(\\boxed{n}\\).ed\n\\end{document}"}]
