\\rfloor^2$.\nassistant\n", "response": "To evaluate \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{17} \\rfloor^2\\), we need to determine the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(\\sqrt{17}\\), and then square that integer.\n\nFirst, we find the approximate value of \\(\\sqrt{17}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16} = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\nSince \\(16 < 17 < 25\\), it follows that \\(4 < \\sqrt{17} < 5\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine if \\(\\sqrt{17}\\) is closer to 4 or 5. We can check the squares of numbers between 4 and 5:\n\\[\n4.1^2 = 16.81 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4.2^2 = 17.64\n\\]\nSince \\(16.81 < 17 < 17.64\\), it follows that \\(4.1 < \\sqrt{17} < 4.2\\).\n\nTherefore, the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(\\sqrt{17}\\) is 4. So, \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{17} \\rfloor = 4\\).\n\nFinally, we square this integer:\n\\[\n\\lfloor \\sqrt{17} \\rfloor^2 = 4^2 = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{17} \\rfloor^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).ed\n\\end{document}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Find the limit of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the limit of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\), we can use Stirling's approximation, which states that \\( n! \\approx \\sqrt{2 \\pi n} \\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n \\) for large \\( n \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\( \\sqrt[n]{n!} \\) using Stirling's approximation:\n\\[ n! \\approx \\sqrt{2 \\pi n} \\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n. \\]\nTaking the \\( n \\)-th root of both sides, we get:\n\\[ \\sqrt[n]{n!} \\approx \\sqrt[n]{\\sqrt{2 \\pi n} \\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n} = \\sqrt[n]{\\sqrt{2 \\pi n}} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{\\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n} = (2 \\pi n)^{1/(2n)} \\cdot \\frac{n}{e}. \\]\nAs \\( n \\to \\infty \\), \\( (2 \\pi n)^{1/(2n)} \\to 1 \\), so we have:\n\\[ \\sqrt[n]{n!} \\approx \\frac{n}{e}. \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\approx \\frac{\\frac{n}{e}}{n} = \\frac{1}{e}. \\]\nThus, the limit of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\) is \\( \\frac{1}{e} \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\( \\boxed{\\frac{1}{e}} \\).ed\n\\end{document}"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Find the limit of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\).\nassistant\n", "response": "To find the limit of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\), we can use Stirling's approximation, which states that \\( n! \\approx \\sqrt{2\\pi n} \\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n \\) for large \\( n \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\( \\sqrt[n]{n!} \\) using Stirling's approximation:\n\\[ n! \\approx \\sqrt{2\\pi n} \\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n. \\]\nTaking the \\( n \\)-th root of both sides, we get:\n\\[ \\sqrt[n]{n!} \\approx \\sqrt[n]{\\sqrt{2\\pi n} \\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n} = \\sqrt[n]{\\sqrt{2\\pi n}} \\cdot \\sqrt[n]{\\left(\\frac{n}{e}\\right)^n} = (2\\pi n)^{1/(2n)} \\cdot \\frac{n}{e}. \\]\nAs \\( n \\to \\infty \\), \\( (2\\pi n)^{1/(2n)} \\to 1 \\), so we have:\n\\[ \\sqrt[n]{n!} \\approx \\frac{n}{e}. \\]\nTherefore,\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\approx \\frac{\\frac{n}{e}}{n} = \\frac{1}{e}. \\]\nThus, the limit of \\( \\frac{\\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n} \\) as \\( n \\to \\infty \\) is \\( \\frac{1}{e} \\).\n\nThe final answer is \\( \\boxed{\\frac{1}{e}} \\).ed\n\\end{document}"}]
