d{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Gary can be Cheerful (1), So-so (2), or Glum (3). The following transition matrix describes the Markov chain that records Gary's mood changes:\n\n$$P=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.4 & 0.1\\\\0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3\\\\0.2 & 0.3 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nGiven the stationary distribution vector:\n\n$$ \\pi = (0.33871,0.370968,0.290323)$$\n\nIf Gary is cheerful today, calculate the expected number of days until he is glum three days in a row.\nassistant\n", "response": "To calculate the expected number of days until Gary is glum three days in a row, we need to consider the Markov chain and the given transition matrix. Let's denote the states as follows:\n- \\( C \\) for Cheerful (1)\n- \\( S \\) for So-so (2)\n- \\( G \\) for Glum (3)\n\nWe are given the transition matrix \\( P \\):\n\n\\[ P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix} \\]\n\nWe need to find the expected number of days until Gary is glum three days in a row. Let's define the following states:\n- \\( G_0 \\): Gary is glum 0 days in a row.\n- \\( G_1 \\): Gary is glum 1 day in a row.\n- \\( G_2 \\): Gary is glum 2 days in a row.\n- \\( G_3 \\): Gary is glum 3 days in a row.\n\nWe are interested in the expected number of days to reach \\( G_3 \\) starting from \\( G_0 \\). Let \\( E_i \\) be the expected number of days to reach \\( G_3 \\) starting from state \\( G_i \\). We need to find \\( E_0 \\).\n\nThe transition probabilities between these states are:\n- From \\( G_0 \\) to \\( G_1 \\): \\( P(G \\to G) = 0.5 \\)\n- From \\( G_1 \\) to \\( G_2 \\): \\( P(G \\to G) = 0.5 \\)\n- From \\( G_2 \\) to \\( G_3 \\): \\( P(G \\to G) = 0.5 \\)\n\nThe expected number of days to reach \\( G_3 \\) from each state can be written as:\n\\[ E_0 = 1 + 0.5E_1 + 0.5E"}, {"prompt": "system\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}.\nuser\nReturn your final response within \\boxed{}. Gary can be Cheerful (1), So-so (2), or Glum (3). The following transition matrix describes the Markov chain that records Gary's mood changes:\n\n$$P=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0.4 & 0.1\\\\0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3\\\\0.2 & 0.3 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nGiven the stationary distribution vector:\n\n$$ \\pi = (0.33871,0.370968,0.290323)$$\n\nIf Gary is cheerful today, calculate the expected number of days until he is glum three days in a row.\nassistant\n", "response": "To calculate the expected number of days until Gary is glum three days in a row, we need to use the concept of first passage times in Markov chains. Let's denote the states as \\( C \\) (Cheerful), \\( S \\) (So-so), and \\( G \\) (Glum).\n\nWe are interested in the expected number of days until Gary is in the state \\( G \\) for three consecutive days, starting from the state \\( C \\).\n\nFirst, we define the following random variables:\n- \\( T_C \\): The expected number of days until Gary is glum three days in a row, starting from \\( C \\).\n- \\( T_S \\): The expected number of days until Gary is glum three days in a row, starting from \\( S \\).\n- \\( T_G \\): The expected number of days until Gary is glum three days in a row, starting from \\( G \\).\n\nWe need to find \\( T_C \\).\n\nWe can set up a system of equations based on the transition probabilities. Let's denote the state of being glum for \\( k \\) consecutive days as \\( G_k \\). We are interested in \\( G_3 \\).\n\nThe equations are as follows:\n1. \\( T_C = 1 + 0.5T_C + 0.4T_S + 0.1T_G \\)\n2. \\( T_S = 1 + 0.3T_C + 0.4T_S + 0.3T_G \\)\n3. \\( T_G = 1 + 0.2T_C + 0.3T_S + 0.5T_G \\)\n\nWe also need to consider the states \\( G_1 \\) and \\( G_2 \\):\n- \\( T_{G_1} = 1 + 0.2T_C + 0.3T_S + 0.5T_{G_2} \\)\n- \\( T_{G_2} = 1 + 0.2T_C + 0.3T_S +"}]
